ere presented to  him  courtesy of Thomas Jefferson but from  zoobel and Pike on one of his Western  expeditions the the Cubs were very cute  at first but then of course they grew up  and became a little bit more unruly and  sadly when one of them escaped peel had  to kill it and then kill its sibling  Titian peel made this drawing of them  after they were mounted for display and  then that picture was in turn copied by  Thomas Doughty  and published and disseminated much more  widely through the cabinet of Natural  History of a popular magazine of the  period so Peale had a huge effect even  to those who didn't have the chance to  actually visit his Museum in  Philadelphia visual imagery was of  course the the language of exchange  internationally during that period and I  show just as an example George Cuvier  his plate of Mastodon teeth so that this  dialogue went both ways across the  Atlantic specimens were at the heart of  these these discussions and the Academy  of Natural Sciences which was founded in  1812 the same time peels museum was was  operating was very much focused on the  specimens and on the scientific exchange  of information and I wanted to  differentiate between the two and you'll  see as they go along as peels museum  actually began to decline from the 1820s  into the 40s when it was finally  dissolved the Academy of Natural  Sciences was moving in the opposite  direction it was growing in numbers and  particularly in the size of its  collections and I show you here just a  range of some of the images the  buildings that the Academy occupied from  its founding in 1812 up until its  current building built in 1876  unfortunately we don't have as many  interior views of these museum buildings  as I wish we did we do have a few  including interestingly the one on the  upper left-hand corners alleged to be  the earliest interior photograph of a  museum existing this is a daguerreotype  which shows among other people Edgar  Allan Poe who was then doing shell  studies at the academy but as you can  see from all of these images even up  until 1900  the museum was pretty much a collection  of specimens it was case after case  after case arranged systematically for  the use of the members although it was  open to the public from 1827 on the  public entertainment was not the primary  mission of the Academy of Natural  Sciences all of that changed quite  abruptly in 1868 not at the instigation  of the Academy but almost by serendipity  the British artist Benjamin Waterhouse  Hawkins who had made his reputation by  creating the first life-size sculptures  of dinosaurs for the Crystal Palace in  London in 1853 came to America  specifically to New York to build a  Paleozoic museum as it was called and  since there were no other experts on  dinosaurs at the time he came to  Philadelphia and spent a good deal of  time at the academy and thanks for all  the help that he received there he  presented the Academy with a fully  articulated dinosaur skeleton the  skeleton had been found by Academy  members and described by Joseph lighty  at the academy Waterhouse Hawkins  thought that it would be good to show it  in its lifelike posture so you see him  there underneath the sculpture on the  left-hand side this is a bit of a  take-off from Charles Wilson Peale zone  erection of the mastodon skeleton in his  museum but surprisingly 1868 this was  the first articulated dinosaur to be  seen in any museum anywhere in the world  and the effect was enormous in the first  year after it was put on display a  hundred thousand visitors came to the  museum to see it and this was at a time  when the museum was only open to the  public for two afternoons a week and it  was closed for the month of August and  July July and August so we're talking  about 40 or 50 days and a hundred  thousand people coming to visit it art  had clearly taken science to a new level  and made it a popular phenomenon the end  of the 19th century the Academy  commissioned the blaschkas  studio the same ones known for having  made the glass flowers at Harvard to  create a series of displays of  invertebrates marine invertebrates in  this case an anemone which were  dazzlingly butyl  and we look at them today as works of  art I'm sure the the curators who  commissioned this work to be done saw  them simply as glass reproductions of  scientific specimens but it's it's an it  raises this interesting overlap of where  as where does art began in science and  in the 20th century the Academy changed  the superficial exterior appearance in  the building  removing the Gothic Revival facade and  replacing it with the sort of Federal  Building as part of the city beautiful  movement and inside dramatic changes  were soon to occur as well from the 18th  from the 1920s through the 60s following  the lead of our younger sister  institution the American Museum in New  York and others the Academy began  developing a series of dioramas and you  see here just a small model by the Jonas  Brothers Studios these projects as we  know well from Steve Quinn's book and  from work Michael Anderson is doing here  at Yale took a great team effort as Amy  refers to it was taxidermists  painters specialists of all kinds to  artificially recreate a lifelike habitat  in three dimensions many of these  dioramas still exist at the academy as  they do here at the Peabody and and at  the American Museum in more recent times  the Academy has focused specifically on  the overlap of science and art by  creating something that we call the art  of Science Gallery and in this space we  do periodic exhibits by creative artists  contemporary artists some of them quite  whimsical in nature others more  seriously scientific one of our speakers  later this afternoon James Prosek will  be exhibiting in the art of Science  Gallery later this year but it's a  wonderful place that brings together the  communities of art and science we also  thought that when we did our  Bicentennial book we wanted the book not  only to reflect the history of the  institution but it's its place in  contemporary America and we commissioned  Rosman Purcell to make photographs from  our collection  now many of you know rosemond's work  she's a boston-based photographer who  work closely with Stephen Jay Gould on a  number of books and many of her own as  well  she's photographed in museums all over  the world but I think she is perhaps the  best of the contemporary photographers  in making scientific specimens of  interest to the art community as well as  to the scientific community  two weeks ago we opened a Bicentennial  exhibit at the Academy which really  brings us full circle it puts specimens  back on display again but I think some  of our founders would be surprised  perhaps even puzzled by the way we've  done this  these were arranged for purely aesthetic  purposes there's no information except  on a separate card about what they are  so as our founders would have arranged  them all taxonomically and had long  explanations of what everything was and  how they related to each other  these were chosen to attract the  interest of the public and and to  reinsert the aesthetic importance of  science into the public dialogue we were  told to keep our presentations very  short so that that's a 200 year history  and didn't in less than 10 minutes but I  invite any of you who would like to come  to Philadelphia and help us continue the  exploration of science and art thank you
 

